phantomos-release
===============

What this package installs
  /usr/share/keyrings/phantomos-archive.gpg — the public key that signs PhantomOS's
  own packages.

What this package does NOT do
  It does not add an apt source. `/etc/apt/sources.list` and everything in
  `/etc/apt/sources.list.d/` on an installed system show Debian's channels and nothing
  of ours, so `apt update` talks to Debian only, and phantomos-* packages have no
  upgrade path from a repository yet.

Why not
  Two reasons, both written down rather than discovered later:
   1. Where the signed repository lives, and who runs it, is an open decision (§12
      item 3 of the spec). A sources entry pointing at a URL that does not exist makes
      `apt update` fail on every machine that installed this package.
   2. The spec's §8 requires an HTTPS-only, signed channel with documented key
      custody. Publishing a channel before that exists would make PhantomOS itself the
      supply-chain risk §8 was written against.

How the packages got here then
  The image is built against a local pool of these metapackages, signed by this key.
  The pool lives on the build host and is *mounted* into the build chroot for the
  duration of the package install, and live-build removes the mount, the sources entry
  and the matching /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d key at the end of that stage — so the pool is
  never part of the image and there is nothing of ours to take out of it. The .debs on
  this machine therefore came from a verified signature at build time, and no
  unverified channel is configured at run time.

Turning a channel on, when M5 decides to
  Create /etc/apt/sources.list.d/phantomos.sources pointing at the hosted repository
  and set Signed-By: /usr/share/keyrings/phantomos-archive.gpg. Nothing else changes.
